  "summary": "The Springfield News-Sun reports that Springfield, Ohio police share access to their Flock ALPR database with about 2,500 outside agencies, including roughly 150 with ICE 287(g) agreements and 18 that have used other Ohio departments' ALPR data for immigration-related searches. Springfield police say they filter out immigration-related queries and internal audits found none, but they denied a records request for audit logs. The article contrasts Springfield with Dayton, which turned off its fixed ALPR cameras, and Kettering, which restricted immigration-related searches.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Springfield police provide about 2,500 outside agencies with access to its Flock automated license plate reader database, including some groups that have cooperation agreements with Immigration and Customs Enforcement",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Records reveal that about 150 of the agencies that have permission to search the Springfield police's Flock database have 287(g) agreements with ICE",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "The Springfield Police Division denied this news outlet's request for audit logs that would show which external agencies have searched the department's ALPR database",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "The city of Dayton recently turned off its fixed-site ALPR cameras after revealing that thousands of external searches of its network were conducted explicitly for immigration-related reasons",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    }
  ],
